 ### [Octahedral Direction Encoding for Gradient Storage](10730-octahedral-direction-encoding-for-gradient-storage.md)
-Octahedral Direction Encoding is a technique to reduce memory usage for precomputed volume gradients by quantizing direction using uniform fractal subdivision of an octahedron into a sphere, rather than storing raw floating-point vectors. It solves the problem of excessive memory consumption in precomputed radiance transfer or similar gradient-based volume rendering methods, where storing full 3D gradient vectors for every voxel is prohibitive. It sets itself apart from naive component-wise quantization by providing a uniform distribution of directions on the sphere, avoiding the clustering artifacts inherent in dividing bits among three independent components.
+Octahedral Direction Encoding is a technique to reduce memory usage for precomputed volume gradients by quantizing direction using uniform fractal subdivision of an octahedron into a sphere, rather than storing raw floating-point vectors. It solves the problem of excessive memory consumption in gradient-based volume rendering methods, where storing full 3D gradient vectors for every voxel is prohibitive. It sets itself apart from naive component-wise quantization by providing a uniform distribution of directions on the sphere, avoiding the clustering artifacts inherent in dividing bits among three independent components.
 
 ### [Gradient Quantization for Shading](14556-gradient-quantization-for-shading.md)
-Gradient Quantization for Shading reduces the precision of gradient direction vectors to a limited bit depth (typically 12–16 bits) to enable shading calculations via a single table lookup, trading geometric accuracy for rendering performance. Unlike [Octahedral Direction Encoding for Gradient Storage](10730-octahedral-direction-encoding-for-gradient-storage.md), which maps directions to a fixed geometric structure, this method relies on the quantization itself to cluster voxels for view-dependent skipping optimizations in Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP).
+Gradient Quantization for Shading is a method that quantizes gradient direction vectors to a limited bit depth (typically 12–16 bits) to enable shading computation via a single table lookup, trading accuracy for rendering performance. It distinguishes itself from its parent method, Gradient Quantization via Polyhedron Tesselation, by focusing specifically on the optimization of the shading term in non-photorealistic volume rendering, and from its sibling Octahedral Direction Encoding for Gradient Storage by utilizing a polyhedral tessellation approach rather than octahedral projection.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The number of vertices grows as $O(4^k)$, and the number of triangles grows as $O(4^k)$. Subdivision at each level requires processing all triangles from the previous level.
-- **Memory Complexity:** The storage required for the quantized gradients is proportional to the number of vertices $g = 30 \cdot 4^k + 2$. Each vertex requires 3 floating-point numbers.
-- **Measured Performance:** For $k=4$, the method yields 7682 vertices, which is sufficient for high-quality directional lighting in volume rendering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
